<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zoltán Tildy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1889">1889</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zoltán Tildy was a Hungarian Reformed pastor and leader of the Independent Smallholders' Party, which won the elections of November 1945. He became prime minister in 1945 and the first president of the Second Hungarian Republic in February 1946, but was forced to resign in 1948 under communist pressure and kept under house arrest. In 1956 he served as a minister of state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-nagy">Imre Nagy</a>'s government and was later imprisoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zoltan-tildy.jpg" alt="Zoltán Tildy" width="320" height="384" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown (MTI photo) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tildy_Zolt%C3%A1n-MTI_1946.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zoltán Tildy was born on 18 November 1889 in Losonc, now Lučenec in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, then in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, into the family of a Hungarian official in the local government. He took a degree in theology at the Reformed Theological Academy in Pápa and then spent a year at Assembly's College in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a>, Ireland. From 1921 he was an active minister of the Reformed Church, and he edited the church's daily paper, Keresztény Család (Christian Family), and other periodicals.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1929 Tildy joined the Independent Smallholders' Party with other noted Hungarian figures, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferenc-nagy">Ferenc Nagy</a>, and soon became its executive vice-president. He was elected to the Hungarian parliament in 1933 and re-elected in 1936 and 1939. He pressed the government of Horthy to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> out of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and after the German occupation he was forced into hiding.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>When the Soviets occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and drove out the Germans, Tildy became leader of the Smallholders' Party and was appointed prime minister, serving from 15 November 1945 until 1 February 1946, when he was elected President of Hungary, the first president of the republic.</p><p>He was forced to resign in July 1948, after allegations emerged that his son-in-law had been arrested for corruption and adultery, and was held under house arrest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> until 1 May 1956. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungarian-revolution-of-1956">Hungarian Revolution of 1956</a> he was appointed a state minister in the coalition government, and when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warsaw-pact">Warsaw Pact</a> intervention crushed the revolution Soviet forces arrested him.</p><p>On 15 June 1958 the Supreme Court sentenced him to six years' imprisonment in the trial of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imre-nagy">Imre Nagy</a> and his associates. He was released under an individual amnesty in April 1959 in view of his advanced years, and lived in complete retirement until his death in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 3 August 1961.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zoltán Tildy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The parliament abolished the monarchy of the Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) and proclaimed a republic on 1 February 1946, with the Smallholder <b>Zoltán Tildy</b> as president and Ferenc Nagy as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/second-hungarian-republic">Second Hungarian Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Lučenec, in what is now <a href="/countries/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.33,19.67&amp;year=1935" aria-label="Lučenec on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Zoltán Tildy"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4416 5945h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4416 5945h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.33,19.67&amp;year=1935">What was here in 1935? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
